<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ionians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ionians were one of the main groups of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, speaking the Ionic dialect and living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and, from about 1000 BCE, the central west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>. Their twelve cities there, including Miletus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> and Chios, formed the Ionian League. Ionia was the birthplace of Greek philosophy and science.</p></div>
